        "thinking": "Three agents have completed:\n1. CF sidecar client + company-skills + agent-instructions - DONE\n2. Platform config axis + R2 log stores + boot wiring - DONE\n3. Route group 2: found the actor blocker - DONE (no changes, but found the issue)\n\nNow I need to:\n1. Update `authz.ts` to accept `RequestCtx` instead of `Request` - in progress above\n2. Update the `index.ts` export of `ActorContext` to include the new types\n3. Re-dispatch route migration agents\n\n\n4. Refactor the authorization functions to work with both the new `RequestCtx` in migrated handlers and the existing Express `Request` in unmigrated routes, since I can't update everything at once. use `req: Request`. \n\nI need to support both until migration is complete. Two options:\n1. Have authz.ts accept a union type `Request | RequestCtx`\n2. Create a new set of functions in `authz.ts` that accept `RequestCtx`, keeping the old ones\n\nThe cleanest approach for migration is to have the authz functions accept `RequestCtx` (the normalized shape). Since the Express adapter now populates all actor fields, `expressHandler` will pass a complete `RequestCtx`. But existing routes that haven't been migrated yet still call `assertBoard(req)` where `req` is Express Request.\n\nDuring the migration period, I can:\n1. Keep existing authz functions accepting `Request`\n2. Add new overloads or separate functions accepting `RequestCtx`\n\nOR, simpler: Since the actor shape on `RequestCtx` (via ActorContext) now matches the actor shape on `Express.Request`, I can make authz functions accept a structural interface that both types satisfy after middleware populates the actor field. This way existing Express routes continue working without changes, and new Handler functions can call the same authz functions with ctx. Looking at what these functions actually access — things like `actor.type`, `actor.source`, `actor.isInstanceAdmin`, and `actor.companyIds` — I can define the interface around those common properties. I'm looking at how to structure the interface so both RequestCtx and Express Request can work with the authorization functions. The cleanest path is to update authz.ts to accept RequestCtx directly, then add compatibility helpers for Express routes that haven't migrated yet, rather than trying to make everything generic. Request for backwards compat during migration\n3. Route agents use the RequestCtx versions\n\nActually, looking at the route migration pattern again — routes are being converted to `Handler` functions, which receive `ctx: RequestCtx`. Inside those handlers, they'll call `assertBoard(ctx)`, etc. The route files still have the outer factory function that gets called with `req: Request` if using Express, but the inner handler body is `async (ctx: RequestCtx) =\u003e {...}`.\n\nSo once routes are migrated to `Handler`, they call authz with `ctx`. Until they're migrated, they call authz with `req`. \n\nThe cleanest solution is to make authz functions accept a discriminated union or use structural typing. I need to define an `AuthzContext` interface that both can satisfy, but Express `Request.actor` isn't typed the same way as `RequestCtx.actor` — it includes the full actor union rather than just `ActorContext | null`.\n\nThe pragmatic approach is to define authz functions to take `RequestCtx` directly, and for existing call sites in un-migrated Express routes, the adapter is already populating the actor correctly, so I can just pass the context through. I'm going back and forth on the best approach here. The structural type idea is clean but feels inelegant, and breaking authz.ts for unmigrated Express routes would create confusion for the parallel migration work happening elsewhere. Let me think through this more carefully — I need a solution that doesn't block other agents or create unexpected compile errors in files they're actively working on. I'm realizing the solution is simpler than I thought — define a structural interface that captures only the fields the authz functions actually use, and both `RequestCtx` and Express `Request` will conform to it at runtime since `actorMiddleware` now populates the full `ActorContext` shape. I'll create an interface with `actor` (typed as `ActorContext` or the \"none\" variant) and `method`, then update the authz functions to accept that instead.\n\nFor `assertAuthenticated`, I need to handle both the null case (from `RequestCtx`) and the \"none\" type (from Express), so the union type in the interface covers both scenarios. Let me check what the Express namespace extension actually declares for `req.actor` to make sure the types align.",
